VALUES (
  '2026/06/30/hdq-month-june-2026',
  'month', 'month',
  'The Month the Peace Deal Set a Record and Then Took It Back', 'On June 17, the US-Iran ceasefire set the TSX all-time high and crashed oil twenty percent in the same session. June closed flat. The interior of that flat number is one of the most violent sector rotations of the year.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX rose 0.25% in June to close at 34,857,</strong><span> a number that conceals one of the most dramatic interior moves of the year: a record closing high of 35,389 on June 16, an intraday all-time peak of 35,629 on June 17, and then a steady give-back as oil crashed and energy names wiped out the gains that had carried the index to its high.</span></li>
<li><strong>WTI crude fell more than 20% in June,</strong><span> its worst monthly performance since late 2021, driven almost entirely by one event: the US-Iran 14-point memorandum of understanding signed June 17, which paused the Hormuz disruption and immediately unleashed a supply repricing that the market had stopped expecting.</span></li>
<li><strong>The June 17 session was the defining moment of the month:</strong><span> Brent crude dropped to below $80 for the first time since March, the TSX printed its intraday all-time high, financials surged on rate-relief hope, and energy names began a decline that erased most of the war premium built since February 28.</span></li>
<li><strong>The GoC 5-year yield fell roughly 13 basis points in June,</strong><span> tracking crude lower as the bond market removed the inflation-from-oil risk premium it had carried since the Hormuz closure, ending the month near 3.01% compared to 3.14% at the May close.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Canadian dollar posted its steepest monthly decline in nearly two years,</strong><span> closing June 30 at 70.40 US cents, down 2.9% for the month, as the CUSMA review deadline approached, the rate differential with the US widened, and speculative positioning turned decisively bearish on the loonie.</span></li>
</ul>',
